This has been accompanied 
by public and critical reflection on humanity's cultural and [[#Legal status|legal relation to the celestial body]], particularly as an object of [[imperialism]], as in the [[space race]], and as an object of [[Space colonization#Colonialism|colonialism]] through [[lunar resources]] [[commercialization of space|commercialization]].<ref name="Alvarez 2020 p."/> The 1970 poem "[[Whitey on the Moon]]" has become famous for its expression of such critical reflections.<ref name="x071">{{cite journal | last1=Simpson | first1=Sid | last2=Weber | first2=Désirée | last3=McMahon | first3=John | title=Towards a critical space theory: The instrumental politics of space exploitation | journal=European Journal of Political Theory | volume=25 | issue=2 | date=2026 | issn=1474-8851 | doi=10.1177/14748851241279679 | pages=269–291 | url=https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/14748851241279679 | access-date=2026-04-20| url-access=subscription }}</ref> Other reflections have invoked the Moon's nature,<ref name="Declaration"/> raising issues like lunar conservation and [[space sustainability|sustainability]],<ref name="Nast 2013">{{cite magazine |title=Space: The Final Frontier of Environmental Disasters? |magazine=[[Wired (magazine)|Wired]] |date=July 15, 2013 |url=https://www.wired.com/2013/07/space-environmentalism/ |access-date=April 9, 2022 |archive-date=July 14, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210714235012/https://www.wired.com/2013/07/space-environmentalism/ |url-status=live}}</ref> understanding the Moon as a [[Commons|common]].<ref name="Open Lunar Foundation 2022">{{cite web |title=Polycentricity for Governance of the Moon as a Commons |website=Open Lunar Foundation |date=March 22, 2022 |url=https://www.openlunar.org/library/polycentricity-for-governance-of-the-moon-as-a-commons |access-date=April 9, 2022 |archive-date=April 20, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220420015444/https://www.openlunar.org/library/polycentricity-for-governance-of-the-moon-as-a-commons |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="The Space Review 2021"/><ref name="Evans 2021"/>

In 2021 the 20 July became the ''International Moon Day'', the date of the [[Apollo 11#Landing|first crewed Moon landing]].<ref name="Nations 1967 a849">{{cite web |author=United Nations |title=International Moon Day |publisher=United Nations |date=July 20, 2021 |url=https://www.un.org/en/observances/moon-day |access-date=November 8, 2023 |archive-date=June 27, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230627042218/https://www.un.org/en/observances/moon-day |url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Lunar effect ===
{{main|Lunar effect}}
The lunar effect is a supposed correlation between specific stages of the roughly 29.5-day lunar cycle, and behavior and physiological changes in humans on Earth. Lunar cycles have significant impacts on human culture, but no solid evidence connects these cycles to human biology.<ref name="FosterRoenneberg2008"/>

The Moon has long been associated with insanity and irrationality; the words ''lunacy'' and ''[[lunatic]]'' are derived from the Latin name for the Moon, ''Luna''. Philosophers [[Aristotle]] and [[Pliny the Elder]] argued that the full moon induced insanity in susceptible individuals, believing that the brain, which is mostly water, must be affected by the Moon and its power over the tides, but the Moon's gravity is too slight to affect any single person.<ref name="sciam"/>
In modern society lunar effects have been claimed to be responsible for supposed increased admissions to psychiatric hospitals, traffic accidents, homicides or suicides during a full moon,<ref name="sciam"/> but these claims have been invalidated by at least 37 studies.<ref name="RottonKelly1985"/>

