'''Makemake'''{{efn|Pronounced as four syllables, with stress on the ''a''{{'}}s. Values of the vowels vary; see infobox.}} ([[minor-planet number]] '''136472''') is a [[dwarf planet]] orbiting the [[Sun]] in the [[Kuiper belt]], a [[circumstellar disc|disc]] of icy bodies beyond the orbit of [[Neptune]]. It is the fourth largest dwarf planet and [[trans-Neptunian object]] in the [[Solar System]], having a diameter 60% that of [[Pluto]]. It was discovered on March 31, 2005, by American astronomers [[Michael E. Brown]], [[Chad Trujillo]], and [[David Rabinowitz]] at [[Palomar Observatory]]. As one of the largest objects found by this team, the discovery of Makemake contributed to the [[IAU definition of planet|reclassification of Pluto as a dwarf planet in 2006]].

Makemake's surface is similar to that of Pluto: it is highly reflective, covered largely by frozen [[methane]], and stained reddish-brown by [[tholin]]s.{{efn|name=red}} Makemake has [[S/2015 (136472) 1|one known moon]], which has not been named. The orbit of this moon suggests that Makemake's rotation has a high [[axial tilt]], which implies that it experiences extreme [[season]]s. Makemake shows evidence of [[geochemistry|geochemical]] activity and [[cryovolcanism]], which has led scientists to suspect that it might harbor a [[subsurface ocean]] of liquid water. Gaseous methane has been detected around Makemake, although it is unclear whether it is contained in an [[atmosphere]] or comes from temporary [[outgassing]].

No high-resolution images of Makemake's surface exist because it has not been visited up close by a [[space probe]]. Makemake is so far from Earth that it appears as a star-like point of light even when viewed through a telescope. Scientists have expressed desire to send a space probe to explore Makemake because of its geological activity and potential subsurface ocean.

== History ==
=== Discovery ===
Makemake was discovered in 2005 by a team of American astronomers consisting of [[Michael E. Brown]], [[Chad Trujillo]], and [[David Rabinowitz]] during their search for [[planets beyond Neptune|large objects beyond the orbit of Neptune]].{{sfn|Schilling|2009|p=200}} The team's search for [[trans-Neptunian object]]s, which began in 2001,{{sfn|Schilling|2009|pp=187–188}} involved routinely imaging the night sky using a [[charge-coupled device]] camera{{efn|The [[charge-coupled device]] camera that discovered Makemake was the [[Quasar Equatorial Survey Team]] (QUEST) camera, which had a ~8.3 square-degree [[field of view]] with a 161-[[megapixel]] resolution.{{sfn|Schwamb}} It was installed onto Palomar Observatory's Samuel Oschin telescope in 2003, when Brown and his team's search for trans-Neptunian objects was still ongoing.{{sfn|Brown (website)|2007}}{{sfn|Schilling|2009|p=191}}}} attached to the {{convert|1.22|m|in|adj=on|sp=us}} [[Samuel Oschin telescope]] at [[Palomar Observatory]] in California, United States.{{sfn|Brown (website)|2007}}{{sfn|Schilling|2009|p=190}} The discovery images of Makemake were taken by this telescope on March 31, 2005,{{sfn|Minor Planet Center}}{{sfn|Green|2005}} but it was not until April 3, 2005, that Brown found the object in his inspection of the images and identified it as exceptionally bright.{{sfn|Brown|2010|p=132}}

Several months before Makemake's discovery, Brown and his team had discovered the exceptionally large trans-Neptunian objects {{dp|Haumea}} and {{dp|Eris}}, which were thought to be at least the size of the then-ninth [[planet]] [[Pluto]].{{sfn|Brown (blog)|2013}} As they were in the process of planning further observations for both objects, the team originally planned to delay the announcement of Makemake to sometime after Eris's planned announcement in October 2005.{{sfn|Schilling|2009|p=202}}{{sfn|Brown|2010|pp=133–134}} However, this plan was upended when a team led by [[José Luis Ortiz Moreno]] at [[Sierra Nevada Observatory]] in Spain [[Controversy over the discovery of Haumea|announced their own discovery of Haumea]] on July 27, 2005.{{sfn|Brown|2010|p=145}}{{sfn|Schilling|2009|p=207}} Brown realized that his team's observing logs containing the positions of Haumea, Eris, and Makemake were unintentionally public and had been accessed by a computer at Ortiz's institution.{{sfn|Brown|2010|pp=154–155}}{{sfn|Schilling|2009|p=211}} Fearing that his team's discoveries of Eris and Makemake would be similarly [[scoop (news)|scooped]], Brown contacted [[Brian G. Marsden]] of the [[Minor Planet Center]] (MPC) on July 29, 2005, to announce their discovery.{{sfn|Hecht|2005}}{{sfn|Brown|2010|p=156}} The MPC issued the discovery announcements for Eris and Makemake on its website at noon [[Pacific Time Zone|California time]], followed by the [[Central Bureau for Astronomical Telegrams]] later that evening.{{sfn|Schilling|2009|p=210}}{{sfn|Marsden|2005}}{{sfn|Green|2005}} The announcement of these Pluto-sized objects prompted widespread debate over [[Definition of planet|what should be considered a planet]],{{sfn|Brown (website)|2007}} which motivated the [[International Astronomical Union]] (IAU) to create [[IAU definition of planet|a new definition of ''planet'']] that reclassified Pluto as a [[dwarf planet]] in August 2006.{{sfn|Barnett|2025}}{{sfn|Space Daily|2008}}

[[File:P48 1994 Jean Large.jpg|thumb|center|Makemake was discovered in images taken by the 1.22-meter [[Samuel Oschin telescope]] at [[Palomar Observatory]] (pictured).|alt=Palomar Observatory's 1.22-meter Samuel Oschin telescope was used to discover Makemake.]]

=== Name and symbol ===
This dwarf planet is named after [[Makemake (deity)|Makemake]], the creator of humanity and [[fertility god|god of fertility]] in the myths of the [[Rapa Nui people]] native to [[Easter Island]].{{sfn|Christensen|2008}} It has the [[minor planet number|minor planet catalog number]] of 136472, which was given by the MPC on September 7, 2005, after the object's orbit became well determined.{{sfn|Sinnott|2005}}{{sfn|Than|2005}} Before Makemake was named, it was known by its [[Provisional designation in astronomy|provisional designation]] {{mp|2005 FY|9}}, which was given by the MPC when its discovery was announced.{{sfn|Marsden|2005}}{{sfn|Christensen|2008}} Makemake was also previously known by its nickname "[[Easter Bunny|Easterbunny]]"{{efn|Science writer [[Govert Schilling]] reported that Brown initially joked about nicknaming Makemake "Dead Pope" as a reference to the then-imminent death of [[Pope John Paul II]], but was dissuaded by his wife Diane and instead opted for the less controversial nickname "Easterbunny".{{sfn|Schilling|2009|p=202}}}} given by Brown's team as a reference to the object's time of discovery shortly after [[Easter]], and by the codename "K05331A" which was automatically assigned by Brown's computer software when he discovered it.{{sfn|Schilling|2009|p=202}}{{sfn|Brown (blog)|2008a}}

In his personal writings and interviews, Brown recounted having difficulty with deciding on a formal name for the dwarf planet, because its known characteristics at the time were not relatable to mythology.{{sfn|Brown (blog)|2008a}}{{sfn|Brown|2010|p=246}}{{sfn|Courtland|2008}}{{sfn|Yeager|2008}} Wanting to preserve the object's connection with Easter, Brown had thought about naming the object after either the [[List of Anglo-Saxon deities|Anglo-Saxon]]  goddess [[Ēostre]] or the [[Anishinaabe traditional beliefs|Anishinaabe]] trickster rabbit [[Manabozho]], but found both names unusable.{{efn|The name of Ēostre has already been used for the asteroid [[343 Ostara]], so it could not be used according to the IAU's rule against duplicate names. For the name of Manabozho, Brown personally found it unappealing because of its [[suffix]] ''[[wikt:bozo|-bozo]]''.{{sfn|Space Daily|2008}}{{sfn|Brown (blog)|2008a}}{{sfn|Brown|2010|p=246}}}} Brown and his team finally settled on the name Makemake, which satisfied both the object's connection with Easter and the IAU's rule for naming [[classical Kuiper belt object]]s after [[creator deity|creator deities]].{{sfn|Space Daily|2008}}{{sfn|International Astronomical Union|loc=Minor Planets}} The name of Makemake was approved and announced by the IAU in July 2008.{{efn|name=iau-announcement|The IAU press release announcing Makemake's naming and classification as a dwarf planet was published on July 19, 2008,{{sfn|Christensen|2008}} but other sources including [[New Scientist]], Space Daily, and [[The Planetary Society]] have reported on this a few days earlier.{{sfn|Courtland|2008}}{{sfn|Space Daily|2008}}{{sfn|Lakdawalla|2008}}}}

A symbol for Makemake {{angbr|[[File:Makemake symbol (fixed width).svg|16px|🝼]]}} was introduced to [[Unicode]] in January 2022, as U+1F77C.{{sfn|Unicode Pipeline}} The use of [[planetary symbol]]s in scientific publications is discouraged by the IAU,{{sfn|Boeck|2018}} so the symbol for Makemake is mostly used by [[astrology|astrologers]].{{sfn|Miller|2021}} However, the symbol was used once by [[NASA]], in an infographic published in 2015.{{sfn|NASA|2015}}{{sfn|Miller|2021|p=4}} The symbol for Makemake was designed by Denis Moskowitz and John T. Whelan; it represents a traditional [[petroglyph]] of Makemake's face, stylized to resemble the letter 'M'.{{sfn|Anderson|2022}} Other astrologers have designed and used their own symbols for Makemake, such as {{angbr|[[File:Makemake symbol (Solar Fire).png|16px]]}}.{{sfn|Miller|2021|p=5}}

== Orbit and classification ==
[[File:Makemake orbit JPL.png|thumb|left|upright=1.25|Diagram showing Makemake's inclined orbit (gray) around the Sun, with the outer planets shown. The vertical gray lines along Makemake's orbital path mark its positions above and below the [[ecliptic]] plane.|alt=A diagram showing the orbits of Makemake and the outer planets. Makemake's orbit is much more tilted compared to the planets.]]

Makemake orbits the Sun beyond [[Neptune]] at an average distance of 45.5&nbsp;[[astronomical unit]]s (AU; {{convert|6.81|e9km|e9mi|abbr=unit|disp=or}}).{{efn|name=barycentric|These orbital elements are expressed in terms of the [[Solar System Barycenter]] (SSB) as the frame of reference.{{sfn|JPL Horizons Barycentric Elements}} Due to planetary [[perturbation (astronomy)|perturbations]], the Sun revolves around the SSB at non-negligible distances, so heliocentric-frame orbital elements and distances (such as those given in [[JPL Small-Body Database|JPL's Small-Body Database]]{{sfn|JPL Small-Body Database Lookup}}) can vary on short timescales.{{sfn|JPL Horizons Heliocentric Elements}}}} It completes one orbit every 307 years.{{efn|name=barycentric}} With an [[orbital eccentricity]] of 0.16, Makemake follows a moderately [[elliptical orbit]]{{sfn|Moltenbrey|2015|pp=212–213}} that comes as close as {{val|38.2|u=AU}} to the Sun ([[perihelion]]) to as far as {{val|52.8|u=AU}} from the Sun (aphelion).{{efn|name=barycentric}} Makemake has a relatively high [[orbital inclination]] of 29° with respect to the [[ecliptic]].{{sfn|JPL Horizons Barycentric Elements}}{{sfn|Moltenbrey|2015|p=212}}

Makemake is currently near aphelion, the farthest point of its orbit.{{sfn|Protopapa et al.|2025|p=9}} It is {{val|52.7|u=AU}} away from the Sun {{as of|November 2025|lc=y}},{{sfn|JPL Horizons 2025-2033}}{{sfn|AstDys}} and will reach aphelion in May 2033.{{sfn|JPL Horizons Aphelion}} Makemake is currently positioned far above the ecliptic{{sfn|Minor Planet Center|2016}}{{sfn|Moltenbrey|2015|p=212}} and will remain so at aphelion, where its [[ecliptic latitude]] will be 25.9°.{{sfn|JPL Horizons Aphelion}} Makemake will cross the ecliptic in 2103{{sfn|JPL Horizons Ecliptic Crossing}} and will come to perihelion –26° below the ecliptic in 2186.{{sfn|JPL Horizons Perihelion}} [[N-body simulation|''N''-body simulations]] show that Makemake's orbit is stable on a scale of billions of years and is unlikely to change significantly over the [[Formation and evolution of the Solar System#Future|remaining life of the Solar System]].{{sfn|Muñoz-Gutiérrez et al.|2021|pp=6–7}}

Makemake shares its orbital characteristics with many other small icy bodies beyond Neptune, which together belong to a region known as the [[Kuiper belt]]. Makemake specifically belongs to the [[classical Kuiper belt object|"dynamically hot" population]] of classical Kuiper belt objects,{{efn|name=hot|"Hot" in this case does not indicate temperature (Makemake is very frigid), but the dynamics of its orbit, which has been highly [[perturbation (astronomy)|perturbed]].}}{{sfn|Moltenbrey|2015|p=212}} whose orbits have high inclinations (''i''&nbsp;>&nbsp;5°), relatively low eccentricities (''e'' < 0.2), and are not in [[orbital resonance]] with Neptune.{{sfn|Barucci|Dalle Ore|Fornasier|2021|p=21}}{{sfn|Schwamb|Brown|Fraser|2014|p=2}} Makemake is the largest member of the classical Kuiper belt,{{sfn|Moltenbrey|2015|p=212}} although it only constitutes a small fraction of the total mass of the belt.{{sfn|Petit|Gladman|Kavelaars|Bannister|2023|p=8}}{{efn|Jean-Marc Petit et al. (2023) estimate that the total mass of the hot classical Kuiper belt is 0.012&nbsp;[[Earth mass]]es ({{val|7.17|e=22|u=kg|p=≈}}).{{sfn|Petit|Gladman|Kavelaars|Bannister|2023|p=8}} Given Makemake's mass of {{val|2.69|e=21|u=kg}},{{sfn|Bamberger|2025}} it is believed to constitute roughly 3.8% of the hot classical Kuiper belt's total mass.}} The hot classical Kuiper belt objects are believed to have been [[gravitational scattering|gravitationally scattered]] by Neptune in the Solar System's early history,{{sfn|Barucci|Dalle Ore|Fornasier|2021|p=22}} hence astronomers have also termed Makemake a "scattered" object.{{sfn|Buie}}{{sfn|Brown|Trujillo|Rabinowitz|2005|p=L98}}{{sfn|Brown et al.|2007|p=284}}

The scientific consensus is that Makemake is a [[dwarf planet]]: that is, that it is massive enough for its [[self-gravitation|own gravity]] to make its shape spherical, but not massive enough to [[clearing the neighbourhood|clear other objects away from its orbit]], as demonstrated by its location in the Kuiper belt.{{sfn|International Astronomical Union|loc=Minor Planets}}{{sfn|Space Daily|2008}} It was the first object named by the IAU under new procedures for naming objects expected to be dwarf planets, and the fourth object announced as a dwarf planet (after the original {{dp|Ceres}}, Pluto, and Eris) since that category had been established in 2006.{{efn|name=iau-announcement}} Makemake is more specifically a [[plutoid]]: the subcategory of dwarf planets that orbit beyond Neptune.{{sfn|Christensen|2008}}{{sfn|Courtland|2008}}

== Size, shape, and mass ==
[[File:Trans-Neptunians Size Albedo Color.svg|thumb|upright=2|Comparison of sizes, albedos, and colors of various large trans-Neptunian objects with diameters greater than {{cvt|700|km|mi}}. Makemake is shown on the top row, second from the right. The dark colored arcs represent uncertainties of the object's size.|alt=An infographic of the largest trans-Neptunian objects, showing a comparison of their diameters, albedos, and colors. Makemake is the fourth largest trans-Neptunian object.]]

Makemake is a nearly spherical object with an [[mean diameter|average diameter]] of around {{cvt|1430|km|mi}},{{sfn|Brown|2013|p=2}} which is about 60% ({{frac|3|5}}) the diameter of Pluto{{efn|Pluto has a diameter of {{val|2376|u=km}}.{{sfn|Williams|2024|loc=Pluto Fact Sheet}}}}{{sfn|Koberlein|2024}} or 11% ({{frac|1|9}}) the [[Earth radius|diameter of Earth]].{{sfn|Barnett|2025}} This makes Makemake the fourth-largest known dwarf planet and trans-Neptunian object in the Solar System, after Pluto, Eris, and Haumea.{{sfn|Carney|2025}} Observations of a [[stellar occultation]] in 2011 showed that Makemake is slightly [[flattening|oblate or flattened]] at its [[rotation|poles]], with an upper limit in its polar diameter of around {{cvt|1420|km|mi}}{{efn|The exact flattening and tilt of Makemake's poles with respect to Earth's line of sight is not known, so the apparent polar diameter of {{val|1420|+18|-24|u=km}} from the 2011 occultation only represents an upper limit to Makemake's true polar diameter. This is because in a stellar occultation, only the occulting object's [[shadow]] is seen.{{sfn|Brown|2013|p=1}}}} and an equatorial diameter of around {{cvt|1434|km|mi}}.{{sfn|Brown|2013|p=1}} These dimensions are consistent with Makemake having a flattened spherical shape known as a [[Maclaurin spheroid]], which occurs when an object is in [[hydrostatic equilibrium]] (that is, the object's gravity is strong enough to compress it into a sphere) and is deformed by its rotation.{{sfn|Brown|2013|p=2}}{{sfn|Hromakina|2019|p=5}}{{sfn|Kiss et al.|2024|p=12}}

Makemake has a mass of between approximately {{val|2.5|e=21}} and {{val|2.9|e=21|u=kg}}, a number determined from the orbital period and distance of its moon.{{sfn|Bamberger|2025|p=3}} This makes Makemake the fourth-most massive known dwarf planet and trans-Neptunian object in the Solar System, again after Eris, Pluto, and Haumea.{{sfn|Kiss et al.|2019}} Compared to other Solar System objects, Makemake is about 3.7% the [[Lunar mass|mass of Earth's moon]] (or 0.045% the [[Earth mass|mass of Earth]]){{efn|[[Earth mass|Earth has a mass of {{val|5.9722|e=24|u=kg}}]] and its [[Lunar mass|moon has a mass of {{val|7.346|e=22|u=kg}}]]}} and around 20% the [[Pluto#Mass and size|mass of Pluto]].{{efn|Pluto has a mass of {{val|1.303|e=22|u=kg}}.{{sfn|Williams|2024|loc=Pluto Fact Sheet}}}} Given Makemake's mass and average diameter, its average [[surface gravity]] is about {{Gr|2.69|715|2}}&nbsp;m/s<sup>2</sup>{{efn|name=surfgrav|The [[surface gravity]] in meters per second squared (m/s<sup>2</sup>) is calculated according to <math>\frac{GM}{r^2}</math>, where ''G'' {{=}} {{physconst|G|round=4}} is the [[gravitational constant]], ''M'' is Makemake's mass in kilograms, and ''r'' is Makemake's radius in meters.}} (about 3.6% [[Earth gravity]]){{efn|The [[standard gravity|standard surface gravity]] of Earth is approximately ''g''<sub>0</sub> {{=}} {{val|9.81|u=m/s2}}.}} and its surface [[escape velocity]] is about {{V2|2.69|715|2}}&nbsp;km/s.{{efn|name=escvel|The surface [[escape velocity]] in meters per second (m/s) is calculated according to <math>v_{esc} = \sqrt{\frac{2GM}{r}}</math>, where ''G'' {{=}} {{physconst|G|round=4}} is the [[gravitational constant]], ''M'' is Makemake's mass in kilograms, and ''r'' is Makemake's radius in meters.}}{{sfn|Protopapa et al.|2025|p=8}}

== Rotation ==
The [[rotation period]] of Makemake is uncertain, with measurements giving either {{convert|11.4 or 22.8|h|d}} {{as of|2025|lc=y}}.{{sfn|Kiss et al.|2024|pp=2, 7}} These rotation period measurements were made by monitoring changes in Makemake's brightness over time, which is plotted as a [[light curve]].{{sfn|Hromakina|2019}}{{sfn|Kiss et al.|2024|p=2}} Makemake exhibits very little variation in brightness (0.03 [[magnitude (astronomy)|magnitudes]]) presumably due to small [[albedo]] variations across its surface, which makes it difficult for telescopes to measure Makemake's light curve and rotation period.{{sfn|Hromakina|2019|pp=1, 6}} For example, studies prior to 2019 suggested possible rotation periods of 7.77, 11.24, 11.5, and 22.48 hours.{{sfn|Hromakina|2019|p=1}} For measurements {{as of|2025|lc=y}}, it is unclear whether Makemake's brightness peaks once or twice per rotation, so it is unclear whether the rotation period is 11.4 hours or double that value at 22.8 hours.{{sfn|Kiss et al.|2024|p=2}}

The [[axial tilt]] of Makemake has not been measured, although it can be reasonably assumed that its rotation axis is aligned with the pole of its moon's orbit.{{sfn|Parker|Buie|Grundy|Noll|2016|p=4}}{{sfn|Parker|2021|p=16}} In that case, Makemake would have a high axial tilt somewhere between {{val|46|and|78|u=°}} with respect to its orbit around the Sun (or {{val|63|-|87|u=°}} with respect to the ecliptic), with its [[Planetary coordinate system#Ellipsoid of revolution (spheroid)|equator]] facing toward the Sun and Earth (near [[equinox]]) at the time its moon was discovered.{{sfn|Parker|Buie|Grundy|Noll|2016|pp=3–4}} This high axial tilt together with its eccentric orbit can give rise to major seasonal changes in Makemake's surface temperature and terrain, similar to [[Climate of Pluto|those seen on Pluto]].{{sfn|Parker|Buie|Grundy|Noll|2016|pp=4–5}}{{sfn|Parker|2021|p=16}} Makemake's moon was predicted to [[eclipse]] Makemake sometime during 2009–2013 or 2023–2027, so Makemake may have passed equinox during either of those year ranges if its rotation is aligned with its moon's orbit.{{sfn|Bamberger|2025|p=1}}

== Geology ==
=== Surface composition and color ===
[[File:Makemake JWST NIRSpec spectrum.png|thumb|upright=2|The [[near-infrared]] [[electromagnetic spectrum|spectrum]] of Makemake, as measured by the [[James Webb Space Telescope]]. The [[absorption band|absorption signatures]] of [[methane]] ({{chem|CH|4}}) at {{val|1|-|2|u=um}} are very prominent in Makemake's spectrum, which indicates it is very abundant on Makemake's surface. Other [[chemical compound]]s detected on Makemake include [[ethane]] ({{chem|C|2|H|6}}), [[acetylene]] ({{chem|C|2|H|2}}), [[deuterium|deuterated]] methane ({{chem|CH|3|D}}), and possibly [[ethylene]] ({{chem|C|2|H|4}}).{{sfn|Protopapa et al.|2025|p=2}}|alt=A spectrum of Makemake plots its relative brightness over wavelengths of light. Dips in brightness at certain wavelengths represent absorption signatures caused by certain chemical compounds. In near-infrared wavelengths of light, Makemake's spectrum shows absorption signatures caused by frozen methane, ethane, acetylene, deuterated methane, gaseous methane, and possibly ethylene.]]
[[File:Illustration of the dwarf planet Makemake (iau0806a).jpg|thumb|upright=1.25|An artistic illustration of Makemake depicting its uniform, light brown surface and lack of a substantial atmosphere|alt=An artistic illustration of Makemake depicts its uniform, light brown surface and lack of a substantial atmosphere.]]

<!-- introduce composition -->
Because of its great distance from the Sun, Makemake's surface has an extremely low [[planetary equilibrium temperature|temperature]] of {{convert|30 to 40|K|C F|lk=in|0}}{{sfn|Kiss et al.|2024|p=5}}{{sfn|Protopapa et al.|2025|p=17}}—cold enough that some [[volatile (astrogeology)|volatile substances]] like [[methane]] can exist as solid ice.{{sfn|Schaller|Brown|2007}} [[Astronomical spectroscopy]] has shown that the surface of Makemake is dominated by frozen methane, with smaller amounts of long-chain [[hydrocarbon]]s including [[ethane]], [[ethylene]], [[acetylene]], and various high-mass [[alkane]]s like [[propane]].{{sfn|Brown|2015}}{{sfn|Protopapa et al.|2025|p=2}}

In [[visible light]], the surface of Makemake appears very bright and reflective with a [[geometric albedo]] of 82% (more reflective than Pluto),{{sfn|Hromakina|2019|p=7}}{{sfn|Verbiscer et al.|2022|p=5}} suggesting that its methane is freshly [[deposition (phase transition)|deposited]].{{sfn|Parker|2021|p=15}}{{sfn|Grundy et al.|2024|pp=3–4}} Makemake's methane ice is highly [[absorbance|absorbent]] in [[near-infrared]], which indicates that it either exists in the form of unusually large, centimeter-sized pellets, or more likely, thick slabs of [[sintering|sintered]] particles.{{sfn|Barnett|2025}}{{sfn|Perna|Hromakina|Merlin|Ieva|2017|p=3597}}{{sfn|Grundy et al.|2024|p=16}} Meanwhile, [[phase curve (astronomy)|phase curve]] measurements by the ''[[New Horizons]]'' [[spacecraft]] suggest that the [[regolith]] on Makemake's surface consists of smooth grains resembling [[snow]].{{sfn|Verbiscer et al.|2022|p=20}}

<!-- tholins & color -->
The long-chain hydrocarbons on Makemake's surface come from the [[irradiation]] of methane by [[ultraviolet]] sunlight and [[cosmic ray]]s, which [[photodissociation|break down]] the methane and trigger [[photochemistry|photochemical reactions]].{{sfn|Brown|2015|p=1}}{{sfn|Protopapa et al.|2025|pp=2, 9}} These photochemical reactions can cascade: transforming methane into ethane, into ethylene, into acetylene, and so on{{sfn|Perna|Hromakina|Merlin|Ieva|2017|pp=3594–3595}} until it leaves a dark, reddish mixture of complex hydrocarbons, called [[tholin]]s.{{sfn|Brown et al.|2007|p=285}}{{sfn|Grundy et al.|2024|p=4}} These tholins give Makemake a reddish-brownish color,{{efn|name=red|In planetary astronomy, the term "red" is used to describe objects that reflect more light at longer (redder) [[wavelength]]s.{{sfn|Barucci|Brown|Emery|Merlin|2008|p=145}} Astronomers have described Makemake and Pluto as having similar "red" colors to each other,{{sfn|Licandro|Pinilla-Alonso|Pedani|Oliva|2006|p=L38}}{{sfn|Heinze|deLahunta|2009|p=437}} although Pluto appears brown to the [[human eye]].{{sfn|Astronomy Picture of the Day|2024}}}} similar to what has been seen on Pluto.{{sfn|Barnett|2025}}{{sfn|Licandro|Pinilla-Alonso|Pedani|Oliva|2006}} Makemake is less red than Pluto, but is somewhat redder than Eris;{{sfn|Verbiscer et al.|2022|p=5}} the difference in color may be due to differing concentrations of tholins on these dwarf planets.{{sfn|Alvarez-Candal et al.|2020|p=5475}} Although tholins should darken the surface of Makemake, the dwarf planet remains bright because fresh methane ice covers up its tholins.{{sfn|Ortiz|Sicardy|Braga-Ribas|Alvarez-Candal|2012|p=569}}{{sfn|Parker|2021|p=15}}{{sfn|Grundy et al.|2024|pp=3–4}}

<!-- depletions -->
Makemake shares its high abundance of methane ice with Pluto and Eris, but unlike those two, Makemake apparently lacks both [[carbon monoxide]] and [[nitrogen]] ices.{{sfn|Grundy et al.|2024|pp=1–2}} The [[James Webb Space Telescope]] (JWST) could not find these two ices in Makemake's surface, indicating that it contains less than 3% nitrogen and less than 1 [[part per million]] of carbon monoxide.{{sfn|Grundy et al.|2024|p=13}}{{sfn|Protopapa et al.|2025|p=1}} Without nitrogen and carbon monoxide to mix with, methane ice on Makemake remains pure and can grow to large thicknesses or grain sizes.{{sfn|Brown et al.|2007|p=288}}{{sfn|Grundy et al.|2024|p=16}} Makemake's lack of nitrogen is expected, because nitrogen is highly [[volatility (chemistry)|volatile]] and its [[vapor]] can escape from Makemake's gravity more easily than from the stronger gravities of Pluto and Eris.{{sfn|Schaller|Brown|2007|p=287}}{{sfn|Parker|2021|p=16}}{{sfn|Grundy et al.|2024|p=18}} The reason for Makemake's apparent lack of carbon monoxide is less clear: it could have been removed via either [[atmospheric escape]] or [[hydrothermal circulation|hydrothermally]]-driven [[geochemistry|geochemical reactions]] inside Makemake, or Makemake could have somehow formed with low amounts of carbon monoxide.{{sfn|Grundy et al.|2024|p=19}}{{sfn|Emery et al.|2024|p=10}}{{sfn|Mousis|Aaron|Benest Couzinou|Schneeberger|2025|p=5}} Water and [[carbon dioxide]] ices are also apparently absent in Makemake's surface, even though they are common [[refractory]] (non-volatile) materials in Kuiper belt objects; this may be because on Makemake, these ices are completely covered by volatile material like methane and its irradiation products.{{sfn|Perna|Hromakina|Merlin|Ieva|2017|p=3598}}{{sfn|Emery et al.|2024|p=13}}

=== Geography === <!-- surface variations, geography -->
Makemake appears to have a uniform surface with only minor variations in albedo, color, and composition across its [[longitude]]s.{{sfn|Lorenzi|Pinilla-Alonso|Licandro|2015}}{{sfn|Perna|Hromakina|Merlin|Ieva|2017}}{{sfn|Hromakina|2019|pp=1, 6}} This contrasts with the highly mottled surface of Pluto.{{sfn|Parker|2021|p=16}} Whether Makemake exhibits significant latitudinal variations remains unknown, as these can only be detected by observing the dwarf planet from different viewing angles (aspect angles{{efn|name=aspect-angle}}) as it progresses through its orbit around the Sun.{{sfn|Hromakina|2019|pp=6–7}}{{sfn|Kiss et al.|2024|p=8}} Because Makemake orbits slowly, such observations require many years.{{sfn|Hromakina|2019|pp=6–7}}{{sfn|Kiss et al.|2024|p=8}} Between 2006 and 2017, Makemake's aspect angle changed by about 11°, yet no corresponding changes were observed in its [[absolute magnitude#Solar System bodies (H)|absolute magnitude]] or light curve.{{sfn|Hromakina|2019|p=7}} If latitudinal variations do exist, they would likely resemble bands running parallel across Makemake's equator.{{sfn|Parker|2021|p=16}}

Planetary scientists [[William M. Grundy]], [[Alex H. Parker]], and colleagues have hypothesized that Makemake's abundant volatile methane may lead to similar [[Geography of Pluto|geography]] and [[Geology of Pluto|geology]] as Pluto.{{sfn|Parker|2021|p=16}}{{sfn|Grundy et al.|2024|p=4}} If Makemake has seasonal volatile transport processes like Pluto, it could potentially produce a longitudinally uniform band of dark material, akin to Pluto's [[Belton Regio]].{{sfn|Parker|2021|p=16}} Alternatively, if Makemake has a non-global atmosphere that froze onto its surface, its equator could be bright and frost-covered, whereas its poles could be darker.{{sfn|Parker|2021|p=16}} Seasonal [[sublimation (phase transition)|sublimation]] and deposition of methane could potentially produce [[penitente (snow formation)|bladed terrain]] or even thick, [[convection|convecting]] glaciers resembling Pluto's [[Sputnik Planitia]].{{sfn|Grundy et al.|2024|p=4}} Makemake is not expected to have mountains taller than {{cvt|10|km|mi}}.{{sfn|Hromakina|2019|p=6}}

=== Interior and possible geological activity ===
Makemake has a [[bulk density]] of about {{val|1.76|u=g/cm3}} (with an uncertainty of ±{{val|0.17|u=g/cm3}}),{{sfn|Bamberger|2025|p=3}} similar to the trans-Neptunian dwarf planets Pluto, {{dp|Gonggong}}, and {{dp|Quaoar}}.{{sfn|Kiss et al.|2019|p=7}} Like for these dwarf planets, this density suggests that Makemake has an interior mostly made of water ice and rock.{{sfn|Kiss et al.|2019|p=7}}{{sfn|Glein et al.|2024|p=10}} Makemake is large enough that its interior is likely [[planetary differentiation|differentiated]], having a rocky core surrounded by layers of ice.{{sfn|McKinnon|Prialnik|Stern|Coradini|2008|p=230}}{{sfn|Parker|2016|loc=The Planetary Society}}{{sfn|Glein et al.|2024|p=8}} Planetary scientists suspect that Makemake's interior contains enough [[radionuclide]]s and [[Earth's internal heat budget#Primordial heat|primordial heat]] to sustain a [[subsurface ocean|subsurface liquid water ocean]], in the past or potentially even today.{{sfn|Glein et al.|2024}}{{sfn|O'Callaghan|2024}} A high amount of heat inside Makemake could give rise to [[List of geological phenomena|geological phenomena]] such as [[cryovolcanism]].{{sfn|Andrews|2024}}{{sfn|Kiss et al.|2024|p=4}}

Spectroscopy by the JWST has detected heavy [[isotopologue]]s of methane containing [[deuterium]] (D or <sup>2</sup>H) and [[carbon-13]] (<sup>13</sup>C) in Makemake's surface, for which astronomers have determined a deuterium-to-hydrogen (D/H) ratio of {{val|2.9|0.6|e=-4}} and a <sup>13</sup>C/<sup>12</sup>C ratio of {{val|0.010|0.003}}.{{sfn|Grundy et al.|2024}} While Makemake's <sup>13</sup>C/<sup>12</sup>C ratio matches those of other Solar System objects, Makemake's D/H ratio is different: it is much lower than the D/H ratios of methane in [[comet]]s, but is similar to the D/H ratios of water in comets.{{sfn|Grundy et al.|2024}}{{sfn|Benningfield|2024}} Planetary scientists have interpreted Makemake's low D/H ratio as evidence for Makemake having a warm interior with active [[Hydrothermal circulation|hydrothermal]] [[geochemistry]]: Makemake's deuterium-poor methane may have inherited its hydrogen from geochemical reactions in subsurface water, which require high temperatures of {{cvt|150|C|F}} that could only be sustained by heat from Makemake's core.{{sfn|Benningfield|2024}}{{sfn|Glein et al.|2024}} In this scenario, Makemake's subsurface water may either exist in the form of liquid water or convecting solid ice, and internally-produced methane may have been transported to Makemake's surface via [[outgassing]] or cryovolcanic eruptions.{{sfn|Glein et al.|2024}} However, it is still possible that Makemake's deuterium-poor methane may be primordial (originating directly from the [[protosolar nebula]] via [[accretion (astrophysics)|accretion]]), so internal geochemical activity may not be necessary to explain its existence.{{sfn|Mousis|Aaron|Benest Couzinou|Schneeberger|2025}}

Makemake emits an unusually high amount of [[mid-infrared]] radiation compared to [[far-infrared]], which has received various interpretations by astronomers since its first reported detection by the [[Spitzer Space Telescope|''Spitzer'' Space Telescope]] in 2008.{{sfn|Kiss et al.|2024|p=1}} Astronomers initially thought that Makemake's excess mid-infrared emission came from patches of dark, warm terrain mixed with bright, cold terrain (and also from its moon after it was discovered), but this hypothesis could not accurately describe Makemake's infrared emission at different [[wavelength]]s,{{sfn|Kiss et al.|2024|pp=1–2}} nor could it explain Makemake's minuscule brightness variability.{{sfn|NASA Hubble Mission Team|2016}}

In 2025, Csaba Kiss and collaborators proposed that Makemake's excess mid-infrared emission could instead be caused by either a cryovolcanic [[hotspot (geology)|hotspot]] reaching temperatures of about {{cvt|150|K|C F}}, or an orbiting [[ring system|ring]] consisting of tiny carbonaceous dust grains.{{sfn|Kiss et al.|2024|pp=1–2}}{{sfn|O'Callaghan|2024}} The cryovolcanic hotspot scenario is favored because the aforementioned dust ring would quickly destabilize due to solar [[radiation pressure]], although the ring could potentially be replenished if cryovolcanic eruptions are able to eject carbonaceous dust into orbit around Makemake.{{sfn|Kiss et al.|2024|p=6}} The proposed cryovolcanic hotspot may be emitting a similar amount of [[heat energy]] as the south pole geysers of [[Saturn]]'s moon [[Enceladus (moon)|Enceladus]], and it could potentially erupt cryolava containing [[ammonia]] and various [[Salt (chemistry)|salt]]s dissolved in liquid water.{{sfn|Kiss et al.|2024|p=4}}{{sfn|O'Callaghan|2024}} The location of this cryovolcanic hotspot on Makemake's surface is unknown, though it is estimated to cover an area of about {{val|350|u=km2}} ({{val|140|u=mi2}}; equivalent to a ~{{cvt|10|km|mi|disp=or|adj=on}}-radius circle).{{sfn|Kiss et al.|2024|pp=3–4}}

== Atmosphere or outgassing ==
[[File:Makemake CH4 emission Protopapa et al. 2025 Fig 5.jpg|thumb|JWST detection of gaseous methane ({{chem|CH|4}}) [[fluorescence]] in Makemake's near-infrared spectrum (left panel, labeled a). Either an outgassing methane [[coma (comet)|coma]] (b) or a thin methane atmosphere (c) can explain the observed fluorescence.|alt=In Makemake's near-infrared spectrum, a series of brightness spikes is present. This is indicative of gaseous methane fluorescence.]]

Analysis of JWST spectroscopy in 2025 revealed the presence of gaseous methane on Makemake, which [[fluorescence|fluoresces]] in near-infrared due to sunlight absorption.{{sfn|Thomas|2025}}{{sfn|Protopapa et al.|2025}} Makemake is the second trans-Neptunian object confirmed to have gas, after Pluto.{{sfn|Protopapa et al.|2025}}{{sfn|Thomas|2025}} However, it is uncertain whether Makemake's methane gas is contained in a gravitationally bound [[atmosphere]], or is temporarily outgassing (if not escaping) from its surface due to methane ice sublimation or cryovolcanic plumes.{{sfn|Protopapa et al.|2025|p=1}}{{sfn|Thomas|2025}} Makemake is barely massive and cold enough to theoretically hold onto an atmosphere of methane or nitrogen; JWST observations have shown that Makemake does not appear to have nitrogen gas, which indicates most of it had already been lost to atmospheric escape.{{sfn|Protopapa et al.|2025|p=17}}

If Makemake's detected methane gas is entirely contained in a gravitationally bound atmosphere, then the surface [[atmospheric pressure]] would be roughly 10&nbsp;[[bar (unit)|picobars]] (1&nbsp;[[micropascal]]), which is 100 billion times less than [[Standard atmosphere (unit)|Earth's atmospheric pressure]] and 1 million times less than [[Atmosphere of Pluto|Pluto's]].{{sfn|Thomas|2025}} Such an extremely thin atmosphere was not detected in observations of Makemake's 2011 stellar occultation, which supports the occultation finding that Makemake lacks a substantial global atmosphere greater than 4–12&nbsp;nanobars (0.4–1.2&nbsp;millipascals).{{sfn|Protopapa et al.|2025|p=9}}{{sfn|Ortiz|Sicardy|Braga-Ribas|Alvarez-Candal|2012}}{{sfn|Perna|Hromakina|Merlin|Ieva|2017}} The temperature of this putative thin atmosphere would be about {{cvt|40|K|C F}}, which is slightly above the sublimation temperature of methane at this atmosphere's surface pressure. This raises the possibility that Makemake's putative atmosphere may be sustained by the sublimation of surface methane ice.{{sfn|Thomas|2025}}{{sfn|Protopapa et al.|2025|p=9}} As Makemake follows an eccentric orbit, its putative atmosphere may change with distance from the Sun: for example, in the warmer temperatures of perihelion, Makemake may sublimate more methane but may lose some to atmospheric escape.{{sfn|Schaller|Brown|2007|p=L62}}

Alternatively, if the methane gas detected by JWST is coming from outgassing only, then it would suggest that Makemake is releasing roughly {{cvt|266|kg|lbs}} of methane per second from 4–30% of its entire surface area.{{sfn|Protopapa et al.|2025|p=9}} It is unknown if the methane is being outgassed at speeds fast enough to escape Makemake's gravity. If methane gas is escaping, it would form a comet-like [[coma (comet)|coma]] surrounding Makemake.{{sfn|Protopapa et al.|2025|p=8}} The estimated mass loss rate would be comparable to that of Enceladus's water plumes ({{cvt|300|kg/s|lb/s|disp=or}}) and the limited surface area of methane emission could potentially be related to Makemake's proposed cryovolcanic hotspot.{{sfn|Protopapa et al.|2025|p=9}} Cryovolcanic outgassing of methane has been hypothesized to be ubiquitous among trans-Neptunian dwarf planets like Makemake.{{sfn|Menten|Sori|Bramson|2022|p=5}}

== Satellites and potential rings ==
=== S/2015 (136472) 1 ===
{{main|S/2015 (136472) 1}}

[[File:Makemake moon Hubble two images.jpg|thumb|Discovery images of Makemake's moon by the [[Hubble Space Telescope]] from April 2015. The moon was visible on April 27, but had moved and become hidden by April 29.|alt=Hubble Space Telescope images of Makemake's moon S/2015 (136472) 1 show it as a small gray dot positioned right next Makemake, which appears as a larger white dot.]]
[[File:Makemake moon Hubble 2018-2019.gif|thumb|Animated [[time lapse]] of {{nowrap|S/2015 (136472) 1}} orbiting Makemake, as seen by Hubble during 2018–2019. Makemake appears smudged because its glare has been digitally removed to make the moon more visible.|alt=A time lapse of S/2015 (136472) 1 orbiting Makemake shows it moving back and forth about the dwarf planet. The back-and-forth motion of S/2015 (136472) 1 is due to its edge-on orbit from Earth's point of view.]]

Makemake has only one known [[natural satellite]] or moon, which is unnamed with the [[provisional designation in astronomy|provisional designation]] {{nowrap|S/2015 (136472) 1}} and unofficial nickname "MK 2".{{sfn|Parker|Buie|Grundy|Noll|2016}}{{sfn|NASA Hubble Mission Team|2016}} It was discovered by astronomers [[Alex H. Parker]], [[Marc W. Buie]], [[William M. Grundy]], and [[Keith S. Noll]] in [[Hubble Space Telescope]] images taken on April 27, 2015, and was announced on April 26, 2016.{{sfn|Minor Planet Center|2016}} {{nowrap|S/2015 (136472) 1}} is about 1,300 times (7.8 magnitudes) fainter than Makemake in visible light and is suspected to have a very dark surface with a diameter of {{cvt|175|km|mi}} in order to explain some of Makemake's excess mid-infrared radiation.{{sfn|NASA Hubble Mission Team|2016}}{{sfn|Parker|Buie|Grundy|Noll|2016|pp=3–4}} The moon follows a likely [[circular orbit]] around Makemake with an [[orbital period]] of 18 days and a [[semi-major axis]] of {{cvt|22250 ± 780|km|mi}}.{{sfn|Bamberger|2025}}

When {{nowrap|S/2015 (136472) 1}} was discovered, its orbit was oriented nearly edge-on from the point of view of Earth-based observatories, which meant that the moon appeared to pass in front of or behind Makemake.{{sfn|Parker|Buie|Grundy|Noll|2016|p=2}}{{sfn|Bamberger|2025|p=3}} Although this edge-on configuration made it difficult for telescopes to image {{nowrap|S/2015 (136472) 1}},{{sfn|NASA Hubble Mission Team|2016}} it may have allowed the moon to [[eclipse]] and [[occultation|occult]] Makemake.{{sfn|Parker|Buie|Grundy|Noll|2016|p=4}}{{sfn|Parker|2016|loc=The Planetary Society}} It is predicted that the moon may have eclipsed Makemake during 2009–2013, or may be still eclipsing Makemake during 2023–2027.{{sfn|Bamberger|2025|p=1}} No eclipses by {{nowrap|S/2015 (136472) 1}} have been reported {{as of|2025|lc=y}}.{{sfn|Bamberger|2025}}

