=== Possibility of other satellites ===
Imaging observations by the Hubble Space Telescope indicate Makemake does not have additional moons brighter than apparent magnitude 26.9 (~10 magnitudes fainter than Makemake){{efn|The size of an object with a known brightness depends on its albedo. If a satellite 10 magnitudes fainter than Makemake has an albedo of 0.7, its diameter would be roughly {{cvt|16|km|mi}}.{{sfn|Burkhart|Ragozzine|Brown|2016|p=8}} A smaller albedo would correspond to a larger diameter.}} at distances beyond {{cvt|30000|km|mi}}.{{sfn|Burkhart|Ragozzine|Brown|2016|p=8}} Larger moons could be hidden from the view of telescopes if they orbited very close to Makemake.{{sfn|Hromakina|2019|p=6}} The possibility of Makemake having an additional dark moon larger than {{nowrap|S/2015 (136472) 1}} has been discussed by astronomers as a potential solution for Makemake's excess mid-infrared emission and apparently slow rotation,{{sfn|Hromakina|2019|p=6}} but it was disfavored because it required an unrealistically large moon size.{{sfn|Kiss et al.|2024|p=3}}

=== Possibility of rings ===
Makemake does not have any known [[ring system|rings]]. Rings around distant objects are too small and faint to be directly imaged by telescopes, so they would ideally be detected in observations of stellar occultations.{{sfn|Sicardy|Braga-Ribas|Buie|Ortiz|2024|p=27}} However, rings were not detected in Makemake's stellar occultation from 2011. If rings do exist around Makemake, they would likely orbit around its equator in an edge-on configuration like {{nowrap|S/2015 (136472) 1}}, which could have made them missable to astronomers during the 2011 occultation.{{sfn|Kiss et al.|2024|p=5}} The possibility of a ring around Makemake has been explored as a potential solution to Makemake's excess mid-infrared emission, but it was deemed unlikely because the hypothesis would require the ring to be made of extremely small (~{{val|100|u=nm}}) dust particles, which would make it vulnerable to destruction by solar radiation pressure within a decade.{{sfn|Kiss et al.|2024|pp=6, 15}} Nevertheless, it might be possible for Makemake to sustain such a ring if it has [[shepherd moon]]s, a continuous production of dust from colliding particles and small moons, or cryovolcanic eruptions ejecting dust into orbit.{{sfn|Kiss et al.|2024|p=6}}

== Origin ==
Like other dwarf planets in the Kuiper belt, Makemake is believed to have formed early in the Solar System's history, about 4.5 billion years ago.{{sfn|Barnett|2025}}{{sfn|Schmid|2024}} The dwarf planets in the Kuiper belt are hypothesized to have begun as small [[planetesimal]]s, which grew to their present-day sizes by [[accretion (astrophysics)|accreting]] surrounding material and other planetesimals over a few million years.{{sfn|McKinnon|Prialnik|Stern|Coradini|2008|p=214}} The temperature of Makemake's formation environment must have been cold enough for volatiles such as methane to condense into solids and subsequently accrete into the dwarf planet.{{sfn|Grundy et al.|2024|pp=3, 17}}{{sfn|Mousis|Aaron|Benest Couzinou|Schneeberger|2025|p=3}} However, Makemake may have lost some of its primordial methane during accretion because it initially had a smaller mass and a warmer temperature due to frequent [[impact event]]s and greater [[solar irradiance]].{{sfn|Glein et al.|2024|p=9}} It has also been hypothesized that at some point in Makemake's past, a massive collision with another body may have formed its moon {{nowrap|S/2015 (136472) 1}}.{{sfn|Parker|2016|loc=The Planetary Society}}

According to a 2020 hypothesis based on Solar System formation models (an update of the [[Nice model]] from 2005 that first proposed a similar scenario), a few tens of millions of years after the Solar System's formation, [[perturbation (astronomy)|gravitational interactions]] among the giant planets caused Neptune to abruptly [[planetary migration|migrate]] outward into a massive [[circumstellar disk]] between 15 and 30&nbsp;AU from the Sun, [[gravitational scattering|gravitationally scattering]] many of the objects within it.{{sfn|de Sousa Ribeiro et al.|2020}}{{sfn|Moore|McKinnon|2021|p=176}} The model indicates that nearly all Kuiper belt objects including Makemake originally formed closer to the Sun than where they are now, in that circumstellar disk.{{sfn|Moore|McKinnon|2021|pp=175–176}}{{sfn|Glein et al.|2024|p=9}} The scattering of this disk is thought to have produced the present-day [[resonant trans-Neptunian object|resonant]] and "hot"{{efn|name=hot}} classical populations of the Kuiper belt (where Makemake now resides) as well as the [[scattered disk]].{{sfn|Moore|McKinnon|2021|p=176}}

== Observation and exploration ==
=== Observation ===
[[File:Makemake and NGC 4274 SDSS 2004-12-13 annotated.png|thumb|upright=1.2|[[Sloan Digital Sky Survey]] [[precovery]] image of Makemake (circled) above the large galaxy [[NGC 4274]] on December 13, 2004|alt=A 2004 precovery image of Makemake shows it as a white dot amidst other white dots (stars) against the black backdrop of space.]]
<!-- brightness & location in sky -->
In terms of visual [[absolute magnitude#Solar System bodies (H)|absolute magnitude]], Makemake is the third intrinsically brightest known trans-Neptunian object, after Eris and Pluto.{{sfn|MPC Database Search}} Makemake owes its high intrinsic brightness to its large size and highly reflective surface.{{efn|The [[absolute magnitude#Solar System bodies (H)|absolute magnitude]] (H) of a Solar System body is calculated according to the equation <math>H = -5\log_{10}{\left( \frac{D \sqrt{p} }{1329 \mathrm{km}} \right)}</math>, which is rearranged from the original absolute magnitude and albedo (p) to diameter (D) equation <math>D = \frac{1329 \mathrm{km}}{\sqrt{p}} \times 10^{-0.2H}</math> given by Harris & Harris (1997).{{sfn|Harris|Harris|1997|p=451}} In the absolute magnitude equation, larger values for both diameter D and albedo p give a smaller (and thus brighter) value for H. Brown alludes to this relationship in his webpage describing the discovery of Eris, where he mentions that a dwarf planet can appear bright if it is either large, highly reflective, or both.{{sfn|Brown (website)|2007}} Makemake is both large and highly reflective.{{sfn|Hromakina|2019}}}} In terms of visual [[apparent magnitude]], on the other hand, Makemake is the second brightest trans-Neptunian object in the sky after Pluto, owing to its closer distance to the Sun than Eris.{{sfn|Brown (website)|2007}}{{sfn|Heinze|deLahunta|2009}} Makemake reaches a peak brightness of about apparent magnitude 17 when it comes to [[opposition (planets)|opposition]] during March to April,{{sfn|Ford|2025}}{{sfn|JPL Horizons 2005-2030 Ephemeris}} which is bright enough to be visible using a high-end [[amateur astronomy|amateur telescope]].{{sfn|Christensen|2008}} Because Makemake is very far from Earth, it appears very small with an [[angular diameter]] of about 38 [[milliarcsecond]]s,{{sfn|Ortiz|Sicardy|Braga-Ribas|Alvarez-Candal|2012|p=568}} so telescopes cannot resolve it beyond a star-like point of light.{{sfn|Ford|2025}} In the sky, Makemake is located in the [[northern celestial hemisphere|northern]] [[constellation]] [[Coma Berenices]] and has been there since its discovery.{{sfn|JPL Horizons 2005-2030 Ephemeris}} In late 2028, Makemake will move to the constellation [[Boötes]].{{sfn|JPL Horizons 2005-2030 Ephemeris}}

<!-- Precovery paragraph. Please do not put anything related to Makemake's discoverability to Clyde Tombaugh, because that is a dubious claim (see Talk:Makemake#Discoverability to Tombaugh) which originated an older version of this Wikipedia article. Any outside sources that mention this claim very likely obtained it from Wikipedia without additional fact-checking. -->
Despite being one of the brightest trans-Neptunian objects, Makemake was discovered relatively late—well after the discoveries of many fainter trans-Neptunian objects.{{sfn|Moltenbrey|2015|p=212}} This is because Makemake follows a highly inclined orbit that brings it far outside the ecliptic—outside where previous [[sky survey]]s had mainly been searching.{{sfn|Moltenbrey|2015|p=212}}{{sfn|Sheppard et al.|2011|p=1}} Although various sky surveys have serendipitously detected Makemake several years before its discovery, these observations (known as [[precovery|precoveries]]) were not recognized until after the fact.{{sfn|Minor Planet Center}}{{efn|Precoveries from [[Kleť Observatory|Kleť]], [[Palomar Observatory|Palomar]], and [[Haleakala Observatory|Haleakala]] observatories were reported to the Minor Planet Center a few months after Makemake's announcement in 2005,{{sfn|Tichy|Ticha|2007}}{{sfn|Betts|2007}}{{sfn|M.P.S. 138898}} while precoveries from the [[Sloan Digital Sky Survey]] were reported much later in 2015.{{sfn|M.P.S. 598178}}}} The earliest known precovery of Makemake comes from a [[photographic plate#Astronomy|photographic plate]] taken at Palomar Observatory on January 29, 1955, which predates Makemake's discovery by just over 50 years (16% of Makemake's orbital period).{{sfn|Minor Planet Center}}

<!-- occultation paragraph -->
While moving across the sky, Makemake may pass in front of a background star and briefly block out its light from Earth's point of view, resulting in a [[stellar occultation]].{{sfn|European Southern Observatory|2012}} Stellar occultations by Makemake can reveal details such as its shape and potential atmosphere, but are difficult to accurately predict because the dwarf planet's great distance from Earth makes it subject to large uncertainties in its position.{{sfn|Ortiz et al.|2011}}{{sfn|Ortiz|Sicardy|Braga-Ribas|Alvarez-Candal|2012|p=566}} Stellar occultations by Makemake are rare because the dwarf planet is located in a region of the sky with relatively few stars.{{sfn|European Southern Observatory|2012}} {{As of|2025}}, only one stellar occultation by Makemake has been successfully predicted and detected by astronomers.{{sfn|Ortiz|Sicardy|Camargo|Santos-Sanz|2019|p=5}} The first and only observed stellar occultation by Makemake took place on April 23, 2011, which yielded 7 positive detections out of 16 participating telescopes located at sites scattered across [[South America]].{{sfn|European Southern Observatory|2012}}{{sfn|Ortiz|Sicardy|Braga-Ribas|Alvarez-Candal|2012|p=566}}

=== Exploration ===
[[File:Makemake New Horizons enhanced.gif|thumb|upright=1.2|Makemake (indicated with red bars) imaged by the ''[[New Horizons]]'' spacecraft on October 6, 2007|alt=Images of Makemake by the New Horizons spacecraft show it as a gray dot moving with respect to background stars against the black backdrop of space.]]
Makemake has not been visited up close by a [[space probe]], although astronomers and planetary scientists have expressed desire to send one there.{{sfn|Glein et al.|2024}} Makemake has been recognized as an attractive exploration target because it potentially hosts a subsurface ocean with ongoing geological activity.{{sfn|Grundy et al.|2009}}{{sfn|Glein et al.|2024|p=13}} The exploration of a trans-Neptunian object like Makemake would provide insights to the [[formation of the Solar System|formation and evolution of the Solar System]].{{sfn|McGranaghan et al.|2011}}{{sfn|Anderson et al.|2024}}

A 2011 study by Ryan McGranaghan and colleagues calculated that a [[planetary flyby|flyby]] mission to Makemake could take just over 16 years using a Jupiter [[gravity assist]], based on a launch date of August 24, 2036. Makemake would be approximately {{val|52.3|u=AU}} from the Sun when the spacecraft arrives.{{sfn|McGranaghan et al.|2011|p=300}} A 2024 study by the [[University of Tennessee]] suggested that if a flyby mission to Makemake made use of a [[Oberth maneuver|powered]] Jupiter gravity assist, it could reach Makemake within a shorter time duration of 9.6–16.4 years, depending on the spacecraft's payload mass.{{sfn|Anderson et al.|2024|p=17}} A powered Jupiter gravity assist would be most optimal for launch dates of August 22, 2036 and September 27, 2048.{{sfn|Anderson et al.|2024|p=7}}

A 2019 study by Amanda Zangari and collaborators identified several possible flyby trajectories to Makemake for different gravity assists and [[characteristic energy|excess launch energies]]. For launch dates in 2036–2039, a single Jupiter gravity assist could bring a spacecraft to Makemake in 12.8–23.6 or 11.6–19.2 years, respectively.{{sfn|Zangari|Finley|Stern|Tapley|2019|p=922}} A single Saturn gravity assist may provide a faster route for lower-energy launches: for launch dates in 2032–2033 or 2036–2040, a spacecraft could reach Makemake in 19.2–22.5 or 12.8–19.1 years, respectively.{{sfn|Zangari|Finley|Stern|Tapley|2019|pp=923, 925}} For launch dates in 2037–2049, a spacecraft could reach Makemake in 16.8–17.3 years using gravity assists from both Jupiter and Saturn.{{sfn|Zangari|Finley|Stern|Tapley|2019|p=923}}

Makemake was observed from afar by the ''[[New Horizons]]'' spacecraft in October 2007 and January 2017, from distances of 52&nbsp;AU and 70&nbsp;AU, respectively.{{sfn|Verbiscer et al.|2022|p=6}} The spacecraft's outbound trajectory through the Kuiper belt permitted observations of Makemake at high [[phase angle (astronomy)|phase angles]] that are otherwise unobtainable from Earth, which enabled the determination of the light scattering properties and [[phase curve (astronomy)|phase curve]] behavior of Makemake's surface.{{sfn|Verbiscer et al.|2022}}

